PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Round 0 debate centers on four requirements: (1) equal Alex/Tyler control via sole directorships + equal voting shares; (2) Alex salary-only (shares lack dividend rights); (3) Tyler dividends (his shares must allow them); (4) pre-authorized non-voting shares usable for future investors with some economic rights. 

Split: A (3 votes) praised for identical voting/dividend A/B shares + authorized non-voting C, but criticized for giving both dividend rights and empty C economics. B (1 vote) praised for separating dividend rights (A no div, B yes) + usable C, but has drafting error ("voting rights" for B) and may reverse assignment. C (2 votes) strongest on exact dividend separation + sole directors + non-voting C, but C lacks economic rights. D (3 votes) simplest for equal control/salary-dividend flexibility, but fails (4) because only two voting classes authorized. E (2 votes) only option with usable non-voting dividend-bearing C, but adds Tyler's sons as directors, breaking (1).

Key unresolved: whether post-incorp amendment to add non-voting class satisfies (4) under OBCA; whether non-voting shares need economic rights to attract investors. No consensus; need to test each option against all four criteria simultaneously. My prior view: none fully clean, C or B closest if economic-rights flaw ignored.}

