7.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {D remains the structure that best satisfies all four requirements without forcing artificial constraints or relying on economically defective share classes. Alex and Tyler as sole directors plus identical voting rights on the Class A and Class B shares deliver equal operational control. Compensation differentiation is straightforward under Ontario practice: Alex receives salary through a standard employment agreement as director/employee, while Tyler receives dividends on his shares—the OBCA treats these as independent mechanisms and does not require mismatched dividend rights at the share-class level.  

For future passive investors, the OBCA s. 168 amendment process allows the articles to be updated post-incorporation to authorize a properly structured non-voting class carrying economic rights, preserving founder control without pre-authorizing shares that deliver nothing of value. B’s description contains an internal inconsistency on Class B rights that cannot be dismissed as a simple typo without rewriting the option, and its Class C, while non-voting, is not the only viable route when amendment flexibility already exists. C’s pre-authorized Class C explicitly carries zero dividend or liquidation rights, rendering it unusable for any investor and defeating requirement 4 outright; that forces an amendment anyway, erasing any supposed advantage. A, E and similar options either route holdings through unnecessary entities, add extraneous directors, or misalign dividend eligibility.  

D’s simpler two-class capitalization therefore meets every stated goal directly while leveraging the statute’s built-in flexibility, keeping the structure clean and investor-ready when needed.}

